{"id":"u-0462affd-d6ca-4e44-9227-1c5dd1c038b9","src":"community","title":"The Spine-Safe 5-Day Armor Program","author":"Ziad Khaled","url":"https://www.boostcamp.app/users/tboVF7-the-5-day-armor-program","desc":"The 5-Day Armor Program is built for people who want to build visible muscle, get stronger, and train hard without relying on risky barbell lifts.\n\nThis is a machine-focused hypertrophy program designed to build the kind of physique that shows in and out of clothing: broader shoulders, a fuller chest, thicker arms, stronger traps, a wider back, solid legs, and powerful glutes. The goal is not just to look good at the beach. The goal is to look stronger in everyday life.\n\nThis program is also designed with lower-back resilience in mind. It may suit people who are recovering from, or have already recovered from, a lower-back or lumbar spine issue and want to return to structured training in a more controlled way. The program avoids high-risk movements such as deadlifts, barbell squats, barbell chest presses, heavy spinal loading, and loaded spinal flexion. Instead, it uses stable machines, controlled ranges of motion, glute-focused work, core stability, and lower-back support exercises.\n\nRehab-style work is included throughout the week, not thrown in randomly. You will see exercises such as Pallof presses, planks, side planks, McKenzie cobra press-ups, hamstring stretching, hip abductor work, glute training, and Roman chair back extensions. These are included to support better trunk control, glute strength, hip stability, and lower-back confidence.\n\nProgressive overload is the foundation of the program. The goal is to improve gradually by adding reps, increasing weight when ready, improving control, and making each set more effective. This is not ego lifting. Most sets should be challenging but clean, with controlled form and around 1 to 3 reps left in reserve.\n\nThe program is structured across 5 training days per week so volume is spread properly. Instead of cramming everything into long, exhausting sessions, each day has a clear focus. Muscle groups are trained with enough weekly sets to grow, while still allowing proper recovery between sessions.\n\nWeek 8 is a deload week. This is where you reduce the load and/or total sets, keep the movements clean, and allow your joints, muscles, nervous system, and lower back to recover before pushing hard again. The deload is not a wasted week. It is part of the plan.\n\nThe 5-Day Armor Program is best for novice to early-intermediate lifters who want a serious, structured, machine-based plan focused on muscle growth, strength progression, glute development, and long-term consistency.\n\nThis is not a medical rehab program, and anyone with an active injury should follow their doctor or physiotherapist’s advice. But for lifters who want a more controlled, spine-conscious way to build muscle, this program was built with that exact goal in mind.","level":["Novice"],"goals":["Muscle","Strength"],"equipment":["Full Gym"],"minutes":80,"joins":2,"rate":0,"rateCount":0,"updated":"2026-11-02T11:47:08.167Z","variations":[{"name":"default","days":["Lower A","Push","Pull","Lower B","Upper Accessory"],"weeks":[[{"ex":[{"n":"Cycling","sets":[{"sec":300}]},{"n":"Side Leg Swings","sets":[{"r":15,"x":2}]},{"n":"Torso Swing","sets":[{"r":15}]},{"n":"Pallof Press","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":12,"rpe":8,"x":2}],"note":"Anti-rotation, controlled"},{"n":"Leg Press","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":9,"x":4}],"note":"Controlled depth, no lower-back rounding"},{"n":"Seated Hamstring Curl","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":9,"x":4}],"note":"Full stretch, controlled"},{"n":"Hamstring Curl","sets":[{"lo":10,"hi":15,"rpe":9,"x":2}]},{"n":"Hip Abductor (Machine)","sets":[{"lo":15,"hi":20,"rpe":9,"x":3}],"note":"Glute medius priority"},{"n":"Hip Adductor (Machine)","sets":[{"lo":12,"hi":15,"rpe":9,"x":2}],"note":"Controlled tempo"},{"n":"Leg Extension","sets":[{"lo":12,"hi":15,"rpe":9,"x":3}],"note":"Squeeze at